        "John Locke offered an influential theory in 1694. He suggested that what makes you the same person is not the same body or even the same soul, but continuity of consciousness, especially memory. You are the same person as that five-year-old because you can, at least in part, remember being them. Locke imagined a prince's consciousness entering a cobbler's body. He argued the person would go with the memories: it would be the prince, in a new body.",
        "But memory theory has problems. Thomas Reid gave a famous objection. A boy is flogged for stealing apples. As a young officer he remembers the flogging. As an old general he remembers being the officer, but has forgotten the flogging. By Locke's theory the general is the officer, and the officer is the boy, yet the general is not the boy. That seems contradictory. Philosophers since have refined Locke's idea, talking about overlapping chains of memory and psychological connection.",
        "David Hume went further. When he looked inward, he said, he never caught a self, only a stream of perceptions: heat, cold, love, hate. Perhaps the self is just a bundle of experiences with no owner underneath. Strikingly, Buddhist thought had developed a related idea much earlier: anatta, or no-self. In a well-known text, The Questions of King Milinda, the monk Nagasena compares a person to a chariot. Is the chariot the wheels, the axle, the frame? None of them alone, yet the word chariot is a useful label for the parts working together. So too, he says, with a person.",
